woman they are all  essentially childlike and even hard  experienced does not teach them anything  now are you going to ask me to explain  the paradox here  not necessarily i mean you want to go  right ahead uh no i can't yeah uh he was  a complex and contradictory person  guess what i really wanted to ask you  was um  do you expect  journalists now in the world we live in  today to be able to sort through all  this and in the end accept him  and still revere him as one of the great  journalists of all time  uh  you know i i myself in spite of the fact  that i have been a menken buff all my  life  uh and admire him and enjoy him  tremendously you know i i would not say  that i revere him  and i wouldn't expect anybody else to  revere him  uh but you know i i would expect  somebody else and especially journalists  uh to respect him for his tremendous  abilities as a journalist and writer  do you think that a lot of journalists  who would use his name all the time as  being one of their idols as a writer  really knew  the story here that you  tell in this book  no they did not  what's that say about journalists  and their ability to investigate  anything  well perhaps so but you have to remember  that they had no access to this anyway  but you said when we started this that  it should come as no surprise if you'd  ever read menken over the years  yes that's right there are  indications of anti-semitism  and of his feelings about black people  scattered here and there in his  published writings  but you know i i think that most of us  you know i was one of them  uh i just skimmed over this and ignored  it  and did not let it get in the way of our  admiration for him  have you done much  talking about this book have you  traveled much with it or is this thing  just taken off on its own it's pretty  much taken off on its own i've had quite  a few interviews  uh  and news with newspaper people and uh  radio talk shows and on television  what are the questions you're asked the  most often  uh how do i account for the  anti-semitism and the racism  is  if somebody gets this book and looks in  it are there going to be some surprises  based on you know lots i mean i've got  to hear a stack of  paper  all the reviews and everything's been  done but are people going to be  surprised by things that have not even  been touched  i mean are there major stories in here  from your perspective that no one's  written about that  no no i wouldn't say so no no major  stories  you know uh  this is not going to make previous  biographies and studies of making  obsolete you know uh  as i said in my introduction  uh it tells us things about him that we  had not hitherto known but it doesn't  tell us anything basically different  uh  you know what it adds it it adds in  in detail not not in any major  revelations  there's another thing that caught my eye  you say has um  that  the story goes that he once read his own  obituary in the sun files meaning the  baltimore sun files approved it  and directed that a single sentence be  added at the end quote as he got older  he got worse  and then you say this was undoubtedly  true what did you mean by that oh  there's no question that he became very  crotchety  and stubborn and set in his ways  uh  he must not have been an easy person to  get along with  uh you know he  he was one of the great humorists of the  time and i think he was this  in person as well as in print  for the most part i don't think he ever  lost his his good humor  or  his  ability to relate to people in his  uh generosity and politeness too  uh but he did become said in his ways  that there were certain things that you  know he believed  and uh  the rest of the world believed otherwise  which meant that the rest of the world  was wrong and he was right  uh and uh  you know  he just became a  crotchety as i said and said in his ways  you said he was a conservative yes  is there conservative dogma that he  writes about that becomes  mankind generate or is he just is it he  just comments from a conservative point  of view or do you find that  he just comments from a conservative  point of view  uh you know if you could reduce his  political philosophy to a sentence or  two  uh he was in total agreement with thomas  jefferson  that's the best form of government is  that which governs least  uh mankind thought that uh  a government had no business whatever  uh intruding into the lives of its  people the government existed for two  things to repel foreign invaders and to  maintain domestic order and tranquility  and this was the source of his hatred of  franklin d roosevelt  who came along and created yeah uh the  ccc the nra the nlrb  uh you know all these other government  agencies  the social security act  uh welfare  and so on and you know uh all of this to  mankind was anathema  because he didn't believe the government  had any business  in such areas you said when we started  this that he was very german and i'm not  sure he said it that way but that that  meant a lot to him what did that what  does that mean to you how do you  translate that so we can understand what  that means uh menken's whole background  and culture was german  uh  three of his four  grandparents were german  his father and mother were  uh he he grew up  and actually in those days a great deal  of the baltimore population was german  uh he he grew up absorbing german ideas  in german culture  uh the germany that he loved i think  that you know an important distinction  has to be made here  was the germany of bach beethoven  schubert brahms  german beer  this sort of thing  uh so it was hard for him  to feel both in world war one and again  in world war ii  to feel any strong anti-german  sentiments  uh at the same time you know i think it  would be wrong to say that he was  pro-nazi  nothing in his writings gives the  slightest indication that he  approved of what hitler was doing in  germany  it has to be admitted he never wrote  anything against it and he should have  of course  why he didn't  is a mystery that you know no no writer  about him no one who knew him has ever  been able to explain  you point out and and as i look through  the  your book to find it uh there is no  entry around december the 7th 1941  that's right there is no entry around ve  or vj day that's right how come  uh  [Music]  again i don't know  uh there is an entry where he talks  about the the end of the war  i don't know whether it was ve day or  the vj day my impression it was a ve uh  but  the the only uh  the matter with which the entry deals is  the the noise that all the people are  making around the neighborhood  while he was working  celebrating the end of the war did he  ever talk about war  well he talks about it here and there in  the diary yes  but but mainly uh  to the extent that it has has had very  little effect on him it hasn't changed  his way of life very much or conversely  that it's inconveniencing him in this  way or that  last question what impact  has baltimore had both on you and on him  can you tell us  something about baltimore that's  different from other places that you  know  uh  let me try to quote area h.l menken  looks like and it's edited by charles  fetcher  we thank you for joining us thank you  for inviting me  charles fetcher is editor of the diary  of h.l menken you can visit the  publisher's website at  randomhouse.com the book is currently  out of print if you're interested in  purchasing a used copy they're available  from several online booksellers  h.l menken was scheduled to be the focus  of this week's american writers  but due to current events american  writers will not be broadcast this  monday
 

